WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.655
- What do you get when you mix Uber

00:00:01.655 --> 00:00:03.340
with a Hospital?

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.173
Royal Pains.

00:00:04.173 --> 00:00:05.298
Beewoop!

00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:07.965
(hip hop music)

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.083
Oh, ball time.

00:00:13.588 --> 00:00:15.963
All right, good dish, good dish.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.643
Oh, oh, oh.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.510
We have a athlete that's collapsed.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:24.343
What are you gonna do?

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:25.176
Check for a pulse.

00:00:25.176 --> 00:00:26.500
Call for help!

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.950
Call for help, first thing
you do, call for help.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:30.970
- [Dr. Lawson] Alright, call 911!

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.530
- Yes, he pointed the person out.

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:33.970
- I need those two gym bags, right now.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:34.803
- If he-

00:00:34.803 --> 00:00:35.760
- I want some ice from those cooler, okay?

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.123
Move it!

00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:37.956
- Does he have a pulse?

00:00:38.963 --> 00:00:41.836
- [Dr. Lawson] Ice, ice, bring it in!

00:00:41.836 --> 00:00:43.230
Okay, I need to ice his crotch,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.833
his armpits, and his scalp.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:47.960
He can't breathe.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.160
- All right we have to break this little

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:49.993
scene down.

00:00:49.993 --> 00:00:52.450
Athlete collapses while
playing basketball.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.340
He's saying he's not
breathing, it sounds like

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.600
he has no pulse.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.470
At that point, you call help, 911.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.309
Point somebody out, because
if you just yell it,

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:03.810
hoping someone else will call
911, that may not happen.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.060
So point to someone, say, "You call 911!"

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.860
and then right away
chest compressions, chest

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.540
compressions, chest compressions.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.744
Don't push with your arms,
push with your upper body.

00:01:13.744 --> 00:01:16.510
Because then you could use
your weight to your advantage

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.740
and not tire as quickly than
just by using your arms.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.720
When you're pushing you
want to do it to the tune

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:22.947
of Stayin' Alive!

00:01:22.947 --> 00:01:23.921
Stayin' Alive!

00:01:23.921 --> 00:01:26.490
Ah, ah, ah, ah!

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.545
All right, that was bad
singing but good effort.

00:01:28.545 --> 00:01:29.660
(tune of Stayin' Alive!) Chest, chest,

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.710
chest, chest compressions.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:32.543
Sorry.

00:01:32.543 --> 00:01:33.376
- [Female Nurse] Aren't you
off today, Doctor Lawson?

00:01:33.376 --> 00:01:34.410
- [Dr. Lawson] I was.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.888
Status, post cardiac arrest.

00:01:35.888 --> 00:01:38.280
Got his pulse back an opened his airway.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.410
- [Female Nurse] Did
you really use gym bags

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:40.280
for immobilization?

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:41.838
- Yeah, can't wait to tell
him some sweaty boxers

00:01:41.838 --> 00:01:42.940
saved his neck.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.500
- So the thing with opening up his airway,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.648
if the patient is still
basically in a coma state,

00:01:48.648 --> 00:01:50.190
they can't protect their airway.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.940
That's why we do the
Glasgow Coma Scale to see

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:54.950
whether or not the person
can protect their airway,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.768
and if they can't they
should be intubated.

00:01:56.768 --> 00:02:00.050
Because what can happen is
some secretions, whether its

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:03.566
saliva, mucus, vomit, can
get into the mouth and

00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:06.497
then get aspirated into
the airway which will go

00:02:06.497 --> 00:02:09.180
into the lungs and that
will create a pneumonitis,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.042
which is a very serious
condition which causes serious

00:02:12.042 --> 00:02:14.990
inflammation of the
lungs an can be deadly.

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:16.300
- [Dr. Wolf] O2 sat 80.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.850
BP 100 over 60.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.066
- Drop five of versa and 150 of sucs.

00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:23.263
- Dr. Lawson, paramedics
bringing in a 68 year old

00:02:23.263 --> 00:02:24.096
with a STEMI.

00:02:24.096 --> 00:02:24.929
- When?

00:02:24.929 --> 00:02:25.762
- Now.

00:02:25.762 --> 00:02:27.920
- A 68 year old with a STEMI means an

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.753
ST-Elevation MI.

00:02:28.753 --> 00:02:30.799
And ST-Elevation is something that we look

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:33.810
for on an EKG when someone's
having a heart attack.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.830
An MI means myocardial
infarction, just another name

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.360
for a heart attack.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.174
- [Dr. Lawson] And into
the femoral artery.

00:02:40.174 --> 00:02:42.100
(machines beeping)

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.350
And there's the blockage.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.980
You can finish up, I'm gonna go out.

00:02:48.478 --> 00:02:50.786
He's stable, he just needs an angio.

00:02:50.786 --> 00:02:52.593
Dr. Wolf, how's the kid?

00:02:52.593 --> 00:02:55.290
- [Dr. Wolf] The EKG shows
a third degree AV block

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.410
and he's unresponsive to meds.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.370
I placed an external pacer
but I'm not getting capture.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:02:59.320
- What's his BP?

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.650
- [Dr. Wolf] Hovering around 60 systolic.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:01.890
- All right, he needs a wire, stat.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.510
- Is Hank a cardiologist?

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:04.343
I'm so confused.

00:03:04.343 --> 00:03:07.210
What I'm interested in here
is, this young athlete,

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.100
he said he as an AV block,
which is a problem with

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.902
the electricity within
the heart that can cause

00:03:12.902 --> 00:03:14.610
the heart to stop beating.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.389
He's saying that they're
trying to create capture

00:03:17.389 --> 00:03:20.010
which is when they put
a device on the outside

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.710
of the chest that tries to
capture the heart's beat

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.320
and reset it and give
it the proper rhythm.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.635
Now, they're not having success with that

00:03:26.635 --> 00:03:28.346
so he says, "I'm gonna put in a wire."

00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:30.453
essentially what he's
saying is he's gonna go

00:03:30.453 --> 00:03:32.634
from the internal structures and control

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.820
the electricity of the heart that way.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.020
Now, we do this all
the time for pacemakers

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.645
and defibrillators,
basically for all sorts of

00:03:38.645 --> 00:03:40.304
congestive heart failure and electrical

00:03:40.304 --> 00:03:41.690
anomalies of the heart.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.287
- Priority is Mr. Gardner!

00:03:43.287 --> 00:03:45.356
You'll see him all the way through.

00:03:45.356 --> 00:03:48.320
- Gardner is stable, his BP is up,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.160
his sat is 98% on two liters.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.560
The ST segments are normalizing
and his pain is gone.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.179
So, how about today we save
two for the price of one.

00:03:55.179 --> 00:03:56.310
Let's go.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.870
- I mean I totally agree with him here.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.678
Why, what does she want him to do?

00:03:59.678 --> 00:04:02.680
Babysit the other VIP patient?

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.200
What good is that going to do them?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.724
If he did the procedure,
he opened up his arteries,

00:04:06.724 --> 00:04:09.360
his ST segments are
normalizing, which means

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.772
that the EKG is improving,
what else is he gonna do?

00:04:12.772 --> 00:04:14.560
(smooth jazz music)

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.883
Ooh!

00:04:18.272 --> 00:04:19.210
- Wow.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.310
- Still can't stop with the romance

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.600
in these medical shows.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.131
With the making and the (kissing noises).

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:28.675
Medicine is fun, we don't
need the (kissing noises).

00:04:28.675 --> 00:04:29.970
Just saying.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.915
- Let's go give that long
day of yours a happy ending.

00:04:32.915 --> 00:04:34.330
- This is a dirty show.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:35.920
- [Dr. Lawson] I recognized
Mr. Gardner's level

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.620
of blockage, I knew it
had to be relieved and

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.420
I was confident that he
would be successfully

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:45.860
angioplastied and survive,
barring any bad luck.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.578
- Well bad luck rained
and poured, didn't it?

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:50.830
While you had our senior
CT surgeon in another room.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:53.178
- To help me try to
rescue a crashing patient,

00:04:53.178 --> 00:04:55.320
I made a judgment call.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.567
- You made a mistake, a fatal one, and

00:04:57.567 --> 00:04:59.816
that's a shame, Dr. Lawson, because we all

00:04:59.816 --> 00:05:01.760
know you're the most talented physician

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.500
this emergency department
has seen, and your

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.239
star was only on the rise.

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:07.320
- Was?

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.970
- I know for the average
person that may watch this,

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:11.780
and think, "Oh, that
makes sense he got fired."

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.440
To me, it makes no sense at all.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.850
I have no idea what he
did wrong, it looked

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:19.300
like he completed most
of the procedure and then

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:20.776
left the rest of it for another doctor

00:05:20.776 --> 00:05:23.320
and then went to perform
another procedure.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.440
He didn't go home to
hang out, so I'm still

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.280
flabbergasted as to why he got fired,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.243
but whatever, it's clearly
not important for the

00:05:29.243 --> 00:05:31.337
show's story line.

00:05:31.337 --> 00:05:32.580
- [Man In Black Shirt] You
really need to get out of

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.360
this apartment and get
some fresh air, bro.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.193
- In Brooklyn?

00:05:35.193 --> 00:05:36.414
- Honestly it smells
so bad, it smells like

00:05:36.414 --> 00:05:40.200
moose had sex with a bucket
of Chinese food in here.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.033
Like musty.

00:05:41.033 --> 00:05:43.441
And you kind of look like
Jesus and Patrick Dempsey

00:05:43.441 --> 00:05:45.914
had a child and that
child grew older and then

00:05:45.914 --> 00:05:47.204
got really sick.

00:05:47.204 --> 00:05:48.278
- That's weird.

00:05:48.278 --> 00:05:49.940
- Look, look, look this trip is gonna get

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:51.630
you back on your feet, I promise.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:52.463
- Trip?

00:05:52.463 --> 00:05:53.750
What trip?

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:54.650
- Are you kidding?

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.339
To the Hamptons!

00:05:57.339 --> 00:05:59.580
I've been telling you
about this all month!

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.150
- Yeah, and all month I've
been saying, "No way."

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.746
- A lot of medicine up front,

00:06:03.746 --> 00:06:07.037
a lot of no medicine in the middle.

00:06:07.037 --> 00:06:08.140
(retching)

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:09.550
- [Dr. Silver] Clear, clear.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:10.597
Dr. Silver, here.

00:06:10.597 --> 00:06:12.470
When are you damn kids gonna learn how

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:13.501
to hold your drugs?

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:14.400
(laughing)

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.000
- Concierge doctor.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.220
- [Dr. Silver] Start with some oxy?

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.053
Then a little crystal?

00:06:18.053 --> 00:06:19.704
Ripped a few lines and chase a little down

00:06:19.704 --> 00:06:21.390
with champagne.

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:22.940
Having salad, you kids call that, right?

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.577
- What? Ava doesn't do drugs.

00:06:24.577 --> 00:06:27.002
- Why? Because she says she doesn't?

00:06:27.002 --> 00:06:28.754
Newsflash, pretty boy, most drug

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.483
addicts are also liars.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:32.830
Sorry, April, we're gonna get this

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:33.998
straight into your system.

00:06:33.998 --> 00:06:36.959
- Doctor, you may have misdiagnosed.

00:06:36.959 --> 00:06:38.520
- Oh really, who are you?

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.004
- Just a concerned observer.

00:06:40.004 --> 00:06:44.320
- Well I observed nausea,
photosensitivity, disorientation,

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.750
a few of the tell tale
symptoms of a opioid overdose.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:48.800
What are you observing?

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.610
- The same symptoms you are, plus a couple

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:51.443
you're not.

00:06:51.443 --> 00:06:53.720
Like her miotic pupils
and sludge toxidrome.

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:56.590
- I don't understand, he
didn't even look at her eyes.

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.570
How does he know what
her pupils look like?

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:00.750
I mean, it sounds like
he has x-ray vision.

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.081
He can see from across the room.

00:07:02.081 --> 00:07:04.914
Especially in a dark room,
how is he able to see a-

00:07:04.914 --> 00:07:06.670
Not buying it, Hank!

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.493
- [Lifeguard] All right lookout, lookout.

00:07:08.493 --> 00:07:10.440
I've got this from here, guy.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:11.760
- Who are you?

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:12.593
- I'm a lifeguard.

00:07:12.593 --> 00:07:13.860
(laughing)

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.860
- Are you board certified
in clinical toxicology?

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:16.950
- Huh?

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.250
- Help me get her up, Baywatch.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:20.205
- I somehow doubt that this
guy's an interventional

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:22.571
cardiologist, clinical toxicologist.

00:07:22.571 --> 00:07:25.030
How many board
certifications does he have?

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.220
And they're not even ones
that go hand in hand.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.370
Like if you're an ER doctor
and you're a toxicologist,

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.790
I get it, but being an
interventional cardiologist

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:33.293
and a toxicologist, eh.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.713
Those are nice Ferraris.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.580
360 Modena?

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.150
- This isn't my blood,
it's my girlfriends.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.100
Can you come inside?

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.280
- Yeah, yeah sure.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:47.120
- No malpractice insurance, no anything.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.033
Just oh yeah, I'm just gonna
be casually your doctor.

00:07:53.182 --> 00:07:54.015
- [Dr. Lawson] I got you!

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:54.848
- Uh oh, fainting.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:57.638
Is he gonna vomit?

00:07:57.638 --> 00:07:58.866
(retching)

00:07:58.866 --> 00:07:59.699
- [Dr. Lawson] Tucker, Tucker.

00:07:59.699 --> 00:08:00.532
- He's sweating.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:03.273
Ooh!

00:08:04.657 --> 00:08:05.712
- [Libby] Are you okay?

00:08:05.712 --> 00:08:07.049
- Is that the seatbelt mark on his chest?

00:08:07.049 --> 00:08:08.460
(groaning)

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:09.970
- Could have a cardiac contusion,

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.370
which is a bruise of the heart.

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:12.369
- Why didn't you tell me you're

00:08:12.369 --> 00:08:13.202
a hemophiliac?

00:08:13.202 --> 00:08:14.035
- Ooh.

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:15.080
- Dude, my chest is killing me.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:16.490
- Where's your Factor VIII supply?

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:18.080
- Bottom drawer, next to my aquarium.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.425
- So he has a bleeding
disorder that if you

00:08:19.425 --> 00:08:21.332
get into a car accident
or any kind of accident,

00:08:21.332 --> 00:08:26.060
for that matter, you start
bleeding excessively.

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.462
And you're missing one
of the clotting factors,

00:08:28.462 --> 00:08:30.050
specifically Factor VIII.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:31.800
That's what he's talking about here.

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.590
I don't know how he
knows it's specifically

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:35.700
Factor VIII, that's really impressive.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.610
And also how he knows exactly that he's a

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.020
hemophiliac.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.242
Its very impressive diagnostic skills that

00:08:41.242 --> 00:08:45.131
I feel are a little bit
past human abilities.

00:08:45.131 --> 00:08:46.329
- Stay with me pal.

00:08:46.329 --> 00:08:47.162
- I can't breathe.

00:08:47.162 --> 00:08:47.995
- Stay with me.

00:08:47.995 --> 00:08:49.046
Tucker, stay with me.

00:08:49.046 --> 00:08:50.050
Tucker, stay-

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:51.629
(choking)

00:08:51.629 --> 00:08:53.096
Tucker.

00:08:53.096 --> 00:08:55.754
All right, jugular's up,
muffled heart sounds,

00:08:55.754 --> 00:08:58.718
minimal pulses, damn it's a bex.

00:08:58.718 --> 00:08:59.781
- What's happening to him?

00:08:59.781 --> 00:09:00.614
- I don't know what a bex is.

00:09:00.614 --> 00:09:01.961
- [Libby] What do you mean, bex?

00:09:01.961 --> 00:09:02.794
- He probably contused his heart and

00:09:02.794 --> 00:09:03.810
bled into the paracardial center.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.192
- What are you talking about?

00:09:05.192 --> 00:09:06.041
- Look at that!

00:09:06.041 --> 00:09:07.280
(horns)

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.660
- His heart is being squeezed and not

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:09.990
circulating blood to his brain.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:11.130
He needs the fluid drained,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.160
but because of his
hemophilia, I could kill him

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.040
trying to save him.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.881
- This is actually really
similar to what happened

00:09:15.881 --> 00:09:20.180
with The Good Doctor's pilot episode,

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.140
where the patient got
into also an accident

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.670
and blood was pooling
around the heart and we

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.284
were worried about cardiac
tamponade, where the

00:09:26.284 --> 00:09:29.923
heart's trying to beat but
there's a build up of fluid

00:09:29.923 --> 00:09:32.640
in the sac surrounding the
heart, so it has no room

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.024
to beat properly, therefore,
all the vital organs

00:09:35.024 --> 00:09:36.585
are not getting enough blood supply.

00:09:36.585 --> 00:09:38.186
What complicates this case even further,

00:09:38.186 --> 00:09:41.180
I have no idea, again, how
he knows he's a hemophiliac,

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:42.890
is that he's a hemophiliac.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.581
So if he's to do any kind
of procedure like insert

00:09:45.581 --> 00:09:48.478
a needle into the paracardial
sac and take the fluid

00:09:48.478 --> 00:09:51.031
away so that the heart can
beat properly, he can kill

00:09:51.031 --> 00:09:52.803
him because he could bleed out.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:54.933
(laughing)

00:09:54.933 --> 00:09:56.841
- Oh my god, this is so fake, its crazy!

00:09:56.841 --> 00:09:57.674
- He's clotting, he's clotting.

00:09:57.674 --> 00:09:58.507
Give me the pen.

00:10:00.236 --> 00:10:01.069
Okay.

00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:04.469
Tape.

00:10:04.469 --> 00:10:06.292
He's doing great, he's clotting.

00:10:06.292 --> 00:10:07.510
- Get it in.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:08.520
- [Dr. Lawson] Okay.

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:09.363
Good, good.

00:10:11.008 --> 00:10:12.310
(gasping)

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:13.143
- Its okay.

00:10:13.143 --> 00:10:14.220
- Ow, ow!

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:15.053
- Tucker!

00:10:15.053 --> 00:10:16.750
- Just take it easy pal, take it easy.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.630
- Take it easy, I just cut
open into your heart and

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.560
your ribs, you're all good
though, just take it easy.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.639
- [Libby] He saved you, he
saved you that's what he did.

00:10:23.639 --> 00:10:24.472
- What?!

00:10:24.472 --> 00:10:25.960
- Libby helped out big time.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:27.130
- Libby helped out?!

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:29.220
My man, call 911.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:31.024
This show is really
funny, I actually enjoyed

00:10:31.024 --> 00:10:33.266
watching it as a regular show, not just

00:10:33.266 --> 00:10:34.720
as a medical drama.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.660
There's obviously some crazy, unrealistic

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:37.992
situations that go on.

00:10:37.992 --> 00:10:40.771
But the one part that is true, is VIP

00:10:40.771 --> 00:10:43.239
Concierge Doctors are all the rage.

00:10:43.239 --> 00:10:46.330
What my goal was when I
was in medical school,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:50.140
was to become a VIP Concierge
Doctor, but for everybody.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:52.430
Make it as a low-cost option.

00:10:52.430 --> 00:10:54.480
And that's sort of coming
into existence with

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.710
something known as Direct Primary Care,

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:58.120
and I'm kind of excited about it.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:00.390
Click here for my three favorite videos

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:01.810
from the last few months.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.318
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:11:04.318 --> 00:11:07.318
(R&amp;B music playing)

